# COMS6200 — LLM-Based Network Troubleshooting Agent

Evaluate a tool-using LLM for diagnosis/localisation of controlled SDN faults versus a deterministic scripted baseline.

Research question: How accurately and efficiently can a tool-using LLM agent diagnose and localise common faults in an OpenFlow-controlled Mininet network compared with a deterministic scripted troubleshooting baseline?

## Current architecture

```
Mininet / OVS → OpenFlow 1.3 → ONOS 2.7.0
  → read-only REST/controller state → Telemetry / Tool Gateway
       ↙ LLM Agent          Scripted Baseline ↘
                    Diagnosis → Evaluator / Logger
                              → optional constrained repair
```

- Python, Mininet, Open vSwitch, OpenFlow 1.3, Layer 2, `10.0.0.0/24`
- Controller: ONOS 2.7.0 in the version-pinned Docker image `onosproject/onos:2.7.0`
- One OpenAI tool-using agent and one deterministic scripted baseline (both planned)
- Ubuntu 24.04.4 is the verified reference Linux environment

The LLM must not receive: unrestricted shell; ONOS credentials; unrestricted ONOS REST; fault labels; injector configuration; hidden ground truth. The telemetry gateway is the only agent-facing interface.

